## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, beginner-friendly security planning around vendor integrations, token binding, and incident response in a multi-tenant setup.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation
- Audience-bound tokens
- API gateway introspection
- Secrets scanning in CI
- IR playbooks

## Code Example

```javascript
// sample token audience check
function authorize(token, audience, tenantId){
  const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, { audience });
  if (payload.tenant_id !== tenantId) throw new Error('Tenant mismatch');
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate per-tenant API keys and verify their use in logs?
- What metrics indicate cross-tenant access was prevented?

## Why This Is Asked

To assess practical control-plane hardening choices under real-world friction, focusing on mobile app token misuse and multi-tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- OAuth2/OIDC security best practices (PKCE, short TTLs)
- Token binding and device fingerprinting
- Token rotation and per-tenant scopes
- Token introspection and revocation
- Observability and IR readiness

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rotate refresh token on use and bind to device and tenant
function onRefresh(token, deviceId, tenant) {
  if (!isValidDevice(token.bindInfo, deviceId, tenant)) throw new Error('invalid');
  const newToken = issueAccessToken(tenant, token.tenant);
  rotateRefreshToken(token.id);
  return { accessToken: newToken, refreshToken: getRotatedRefresh(token.id) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the token binding mechanism in CI/CD?
- What metrics indicate token abuse and how would you respond?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical application of confidential computing to multi-tenant isolation in real-time ML workloads, beyond token-based controls.

## Key Concepts
- Confidential computing (TDX/Nitro/SEV)
- Per-tenant attested enclaves and key management
- Envelope encryption of in-flight data and outputs
- Attestation-based admission control at API gateway
- IR playbook with measurable success criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-spec showing attestation flow
function attestedInference(req, enclave) {
  if (!enclave.attest(req.tenant)) throw new Error('Untrusted tenant');
  const input = enclave.declassify(req.input, req.tenant);
  const out = enclave.infer(input);
  return enclave.envelope(out, req.tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate per-tenant keys without service downtime?
- How would you detect and respond to a compromised enclave?

## Why This Is Asked
Examines practical isolation controls for logs in multi-tenant cloud-native systems, including data-minimization, policy enforcement, and incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped logging
- ABAC/OPA policy enforcement at ingestion
- Data redaction and least-privilege exporters
- SPIRE/mTLS between log agents and collectors
- Ephemeral credentials rotation and secrets hygiene

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactForTenant(log, tenantId) {
  const redacted = {...log};
  const sensitiveKeys = ['ssn','creditCard','dob','email'];
  for (const k of sensitiveKeys) {
    if (redacted[k] && redacted.tenantId === tenantId) {
      redacted[k] = '[REDACTED]';
    }
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for cross-tenant access in search results?
- What monitoring signals indicate redaction failures or policy drift?

Why This Is Asked
Designing robust data access for a Snowflake/MongoDB data mesh with external BI access and cross-tenant workloads is critical. The scenario requires concrete controls beyond high-level security principles, including policy-as-code, data masking, and rapid IR.

Key Concepts
- Snowflake RBAC and schema-level grants
- Dynamic data masking and row-level security
- Data classification and policy-as-code (Terraform/OPA)
- BI-tool access via OIDC, short-lived tokens, and IP allowlists
- Access History, risk alerts, and SIEM integration

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-authorization check
if (user.tenant !== data.ownerTenant || !user.roles.includes('tenant_read')) {
  throw new Error('Access denied');
}
if (data.classification === 'PII') {
  applyMasking(data);
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you write tests to validate masking rules across datasets?
- How would you evolve policy-as-code as new datasets are added?
- How would you detect and respond to anomalous data access in real time?
